When another world reads an object-valued event payload, Ladybird
returns a clone of it made in the reader’s realm, or null when the
value can’t be cloned. Blink does the same for CustomEvent::detail() and
MessageEvent::data(), through WorldSafeV8Reference::GetAcrossWorld(),
but its ErrorEvent::error() returns null across worlds, where Ladybird
returns a clone. WebKit’s JSCustomEvent::detail(), JSErrorEvent::
error(), JSMessageEvent::data(), JSPopStateEvent::state(), and
JSHistory::state() all clone through cachedPropertyValue() and
cloneAcrossWorlds(). Gecko has no clones here: Its compartment wrappers
stand in for the object, and AccessCheck::wrapperSubsumes() decides
what the reader may touch.

History.state keeps one clone per reading world, as Blink’s History::
StateHelper() caches the state on the world’s own wrapper and WebKit’s
JSHistory::state() caches it too. And an uncaught error stays in
its world: Blink’s ErrorEvent::CanBeDispatchedInWorld() limits
its dispatch the same way.
